phil hays wrote:
 > Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

 >> The thing is, with ever growing computer hardware
 >> platform capabilities, use of software overlays
 >> has quite a bit gone out of style today, though
 >> I'm betting there are some embedded computer
 >> systems that still use them with overlays read
 >> from firmware instead of from disk or tape.

 > Suppose you have a overlay size of 2 KB. If a bug
 > fix increases the size from 2047 bytes to 2049
 > bytes, you are so screwed...

And yet I used overlayed code (in Fortran 77) to help
predict tropical cyclone motion, on which forecasts
the safety of human lives depended, so the idea
hasn't lost its merit. There's nothing that says you
must, or should, size any overlay exactly to the
maximum overlay area size, either.

 > Been there, done that, have the t-shirt. Never
 > again.

Well, when you are making embedded computer devices
produced in the tens of millions, you do a _lot_ to
keep down the sizes of components whose prices seem
trivial in larger systems produced in smaller lot
sizes, and the amortized software effort per device
stays pretty close to nil no matter how hard you
make the programmer work, compared to having to go
up to the next memory size.

[It tickles me that the same code I worked on is still
creating graphics that are shown on TV for hurricane
predicted path displays these 14 years later. When a
tropical cyclone exists somewhere, the chart labeled
"ATCF" (not always present) on the page for that
tropical cyclone reached on labeled links from this
link:

http://www.nrlmry.navy.mil/tc_pages/tc_home.html

is output from the then-overlaid software, running
in MS-DOS, on which I worked. That software has
since been ported to some Unix-alike (at the time,
HP/UX), but still runs very much the same way,
though the chances it is still run overlaid are
nearly nil.]

